        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I had these:\r\n> \r\n> ...\r\n>\r\n> I don't understand what exactly I achieved in these cryptic vim script snippets, it is written years ago, but it was doing the job.\r\n> \r\n> Yesterday I changed them to these:\r\n> ...\r\n\r\nGood thing this works for you!\r\n\r\nTwo observations here:\r\n- Crucial part here is a call to `vim.fn['repeat#set']` which utilizes 'tpope/vim-repeat' - the core reason for dot-repeat. On that note, `[e` and `]e` in 'vim-unimpaired' are also not dot-repeatable out of the box, only when using 'tpope/vim-repeat'.\r\n- I'd simplify a bit (or rather would have taken different route with) Lua code by using `vim.cmd('normal! xp')` and `vim.cmd('normal! Xph')` (along with caching register and going around some edge cases). It will also work on multi-byte characters where current solutions don't.",
