        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "You are right, both of them work with vim-repeat, but I have a vague impression that the old vim-script version, which is using an `<expr>` mapping used to work in vim-script, before I move it to Lua files and define it using vim.keymap. Or maybe I'm mistaken, maybe I try hard to achieve it with `<expr>` but failed and just kept it as is.\r\n\r\n`xp` and `Xph` are the way most people go, it just ruins the registers as you mentioned, and other than that they don't seem to be repeatable, I tried this:\r\n```Lua\r\nvim.keymap.set(\r\n  'n',\r\n  ']r',\r\n  function()\r\n    vim.cmd([[\r\n    normal! xp\r\n    ]])\r\n  end,\r\n  {}\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\nBtw, the trend these days is to have mini.everything ;) Maybe we can have a mini.repeat. [It is one of the smallest and yet popular vim plugins](https://github.com/tpope/vim-repeat/blob/master/autoload/repeat.vim). I couldn't believe my search for \"repeat.nvim\" or \"nvim-repeat\" in Google returned no result.",
